---
/* What a cap below the floor buys — for "The nudge that sat below the
   median".
   ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The article's counterintuitive half: a too-low ceiling does not save
   tokens, it buys the same expensive orientation again and again while the
   checkpoint count stays where it was. Two rails, same work. On the first, a
   ceiling with headroom: one start-up cost, then the cheap cached middle,
   then the closing steps — and a checkpoint. On the second, a cap below the
   floor: session after session pays full price to orient, is killed before
   the closing steps, and hands the next session the same re-reading to do.

   Same idiom as the other rails drawings: panes, mono labels, arrows that
   are characters, semantic colour for status only. Nothing is to scale — the
   measured tokens-per-checkpoint for every window is in the strip. */

const KILLED_SESSIONS = ["first", "second", "third"];
---

<figure class="fig fig-churn">
  <div
    class="rails"
    role="img"
    aria-label="The same work on two rails. Under a ceiling with headroom, one session pays the start-up cost once — reading the tracker, the plan and the code at full price — then works in cheap cache reads and ends on its own terms: commit, handoff, checkpoint closed. Under a cap below the floor, three sessions in a row each pay the same full-price orientation, are killed at the ceiling before the closing steps, and close nothing — the ledger pays for the expensive tokens repeatedly while the checkpoint count stays where it was."
  >
    <div class="rail" aria-hidden="true">
      <p class="r-label">a ceiling with headroom — the same work, once</p>
      <div class="row">
        <span class="box orient">orient — full price</span>
        <span class="arrow">→</span>
        <span class="box">work — cache reads</span>
        <span class="arrow">→</span>
        <span class="box close">commit · handoff · closed <span class="ok">✓</span></span>
      </div>
      <p class="r-note">one start-up cost, one checkpoint</p>
    </div>

    <div class="rail" aria-hidden="true">
      <p class="r-label">a cap below the floor — the same work, over and over</p>
      {
        KILLED_SESSIONS.map((nth) => (
          <div class="row">
            <span class="box orient">orient — full price{nth === "first" ? "" : ", again"}</span>
            <span class="arrow">→</span>
            <span class="box">work</span>
            <span class="arrow">→</span>
            <span class="box kill">killed at the ceiling</span>
          </div>
        ))
      }
      <p class="r-note bad">
        the expensive tokens, bought three times — and the checkpoint count has not moved
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <figcaption>
    Continuing a long session is cheap; starting a new one is not. A cap set
    below the floor converts that arithmetic into churn: every kill sends the
    next session back to page one at full price, which is why
    tokens-per-checkpoint — the figure a cap is usually justified by — is the
    one that rises.
  </figcaption>
</figure>

<style>
  .rails {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-m);
  }

  .rail {
    background: var(--mantle);
    border: var(--rule);
    padding: var(--space-m) var(--space-m) var(--space-s);
  }

  .r-label {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: var(--text-micro);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    color: var(--muted);
    margin-block-end: var(--space-m);
  }

  .row {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-2xs);
  }

  .row + .row {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-2xs);
  }

  .box {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: var(--text-micro);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    border: var(--rule);
    background: var(--base);
    padding-block: var(--space-2xs);
    padding-inline: var(--space-xs);
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .arrow {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  /* The repeated purchase is the subject, so orientation wears the accent. */
  .box.orient {
    color: var(--current);
  }

  .ok {
    color: var(--green);
  }

  .box.kill {
    color: var(--red);
  }

  .r-note {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-s);
    font-size: var(--text-small);
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .r-note.bad {
    color: var(--red);
  }
</style>
